  "snippet": false,
  "code": "use IO.Std.write_line;\nuse Ghul.Pipes;\n\n// A trait with no bodies is a pure interface: an implementing type has to supply every member.\ntrait Shape is\n    area() -> double;\n    name() -> string;\nsi\n\n// A trait member may carry a default body instead. Implementing types inherit it and need only\n// write the member to say something else.\ntrait Described is\n    describe() -> string => \"a shape\";\nsi\n\n// An abstract class is the partial implementation: it can hold state and finished behaviour, and\n// a member left without a body is what its subclasses have to provide. Naming it abstract is\n// what stops it being constructed, and Polygon() is a compile error rather than a runtime one.\nclass Polygon: Shape, Described abstract is\n    _sides: int;\n\n    init(sides: int) is\n        _sides = sides;\n    si\n\n    area() -> double;\n\n    name() -> string => \"{_sides}-sided polygon\";\n\n    describe() -> string => \"{name()}, area {area():F2}\";\nsi\n\nclass SQUARE: Polygon is\n    _side: double;\n\n    init(side: double) is\n        super.init(4);\n\n        _side = side;\n    si\n\n    area() -> double => _side * _side;\nsi\n\nclass TRIANGLE: Polygon is\n    _base: double;\n    _height: double;\n\n    init(base: double, height: double) is\n        super.init(3);\n\n        _base = base;\n        _height = height;\n    si\n\n    area() -> double => _base * _height / 2.0D;\n\n    // Overriding a default a trait supplied, reaching the original with super.\n    describe() -> string => \"{super.describe()}, and it is a triangle\";\nsi\n\nlet shapes: Shape[] = [SQUARE(3.0D), TRIANGLE(4.0D, 5.0D)];\n\nshapes |> each(shape => write_line(shape.name()));\n\n// Described is a separate contract, satisfied through Polygon, so the same values pass as either.\nlet described: Described[] = [SQUARE(2.0D), TRIANGLE(6.0D, 1.0D)];\n\ndescribed |> each(d => write_line(d.describe()));",
  "fullSource": "use IO.Std.write_line;\nuse Ghul.Pipes;\n\n// A trait with no bodies is a pure interface: an implementing type has to supply every member.\ntrait Shape is\n    area() -> double;\n    name() -> string;\nsi\n\n// A trait member may carry a default body instead. Implementing types inherit it and need only\n// write the member to say something else.\ntrait Described is\n    describe() -> string => \"a shape\";\nsi\n\n// An abstract class is the partial implementation: it can hold state and finished behaviour, and\n// a member left without a body is what its subclasses have to provide. Naming it abstract is\n// what stops it being constructed, and Polygon() is a compile error rather than a runtime one.\nclass Polygon: Shape, Described abstract is\n    _sides: int;\n\n    init(sides: int) is\n        _sides = sides;\n    si\n\n    area() -> double;\n\n    name() -> string => \"{_sides}-sided polygon\";\n\n    describe() -> string => \"{name()}, area {area():F2}\";\nsi\n\nclass SQUARE: Polygon is\n    _side: double;\n\n    init(side: double) is\n        super.init(4);\n\n        _side = side;\n    si\n\n    area() -> double => _side * _side;\nsi\n\nclass TRIANGLE: Polygon is\n    _base: double;\n    _height: double;\n\n    init(base: double, height: double) is\n        super.init(3);\n\n        _base = base;\n        _height = height;\n    si\n\n    area() -> double => _base * _height / 2.0D;\n\n    // Overriding a default a trait supplied, reaching the original with super.\n    describe() -> string => \"{super.describe()}, and it is a triangle\";\nsi\n\nlet shapes: Shape[] = [SQUARE(3.0D), TRIANGLE(4.0D, 5.0D)];\n\nshapes |> each(shape => write_line(shape.name()));\n\n// Described is a separate contract, satisfied through Polygon, so the same values pass as either.\nlet described: Described[] = [SQUARE(2.0D), TRIANGLE(6.0D, 1.0D)];\n\ndescribed |> each(d => write_line(d.describe()));\n",
